# Cloudflare Pages redirects.
#
# The network DIRECTORY is the root `/` now (it lists every watershed-point site). The live
# site (Lima) lives at /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co; coming-soon sites at /network/<slug>;
# the live site's investigation is the *walk* at /walk/project-bosc (guided walk, flattened
# chapters); research moved to /research/hypotheses. These keep every old, deployed URL working.
#
# NETWORK-GLOBAL pages live at the root and are NOT rewritten — /about, /about-me, /wiki/*, /ask,
# /submit (the network-tier lead form), /basin, and the API (/api/*).
#
# IMPORTANT — on Cloudflare Pages a redirect ALWAYS fires before a matching static asset. So a
# broad `/<x>/*` rule shadows real files under that path. Consequences encoded in the order below:
#  - NO rule for `/`: it's the real directory hub now (served statically).
#  - NO `/submit` / `/research` / `/network/*` broad rules: those are real pages now.
#  - `/walk/crops/*` is a 200 passthrough BEFORE `/walk/*` (teardown crops live at public/walk/crops).
#  - the per-directory sub-paths (hypotheses/basin) come BEFORE the `/directory/*`
#    catch-all that maps the old coming-soon URLs onto /network/<slug>.

# Teardown crops — serve as-is before any /walk redirect.
/walk/crops/*           /walk/crops/:splat                                                     200

# The old /directory tree → its new homes. Specific sub-paths first, then the coming-soon catch-all.
/directory/hypotheses   /research/hypotheses                                                   301
/directory/basin        /basin                                                                 301
# The locked-screen preview was retired with the rest of the dev surface (#1894). Its legacy
# address has to stay AHEAD of the catch-all below: `/directory/*` would rewrite it to
# `/network/locked-preview`, and no site is registered under that slug, so the 301 would land on a
# 404. The network index is the nearest live thing — it lists every site and its status, which is
# what the preview was illustrating.
/directory/locked-preview  /network                                                            301
/directory/*            /network/:splat                                                         301
/directory              /                                                                      301

# The two section hubs became lens landings (#1915, epic #1911). Their LEAVES did not move — every
# /environment/<facet> and /economy/<facet> keeps its address — so these rules carry NO `/*`: a
# splat here would fire before the real leaf pages and 301 the whole section away. `:site` matches
# exactly one segment, and the trailing slash is what the retired `index.astro` routes emitted.
/network/:site/environment/   /network/:site/lens/environment                                  301
/network/:site/economy/       /network/:site/lens/economy                                      301

# /bosc -> /network/<id>, and the walk moved to /walk/project-bosc (#1972; chapters flattened).
/bosc/start             /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/walk/project-bosc            301
/bosc/intro             /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/walk/project-bosc            301
/bosc/walk              /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/walk/project-bosc/contents   301
/bosc/walk/*            /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/walk/project-bosc/:splat     301
/bosc                   /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/                                301
/bosc/*                 /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/:splat                          301

# Even-older root-level legacy URLs (pre-/bosc) — straight to the new paths.
/start                  /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/walk/project-bosc            301
/timeline               /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/timeline                        301
/site/*                 /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/site/:splat                     301
/watershed/*            /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/environment/:splat                301
/reports/*              /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/reports/:splat                  301
/walk/*                 /network/american-sugar-creek-allen-co/walk/project-bosc/:splat     301

# The editorial walk left the `/stories/` namespace it shared with the reader-composed Story
# (#1972, epic #1968). Keyed to the CODENAME, never a bare `/stories/*` wildcard — that would
# swallow the UGC pages (compose|mine|read|grab-demo) that legitimately still live there.
/network/:site/stories/project-bosc      /network/:site/walk/project-bosc            301
/network/:site/stories/project-bosc/*    /network/:site/walk/project-bosc/:splat     301

# Docs moved to the network-global `/docs/` build (#1304): the long-form prose is one root build,
# not per-site. The old `/docs/* → per-site` rewrite is gone (it now points nowhere and would shadow
# the real files); keep old per-site docs URLs working by sending them to the global build instead.
/network/:site/docs             /docs/                                                         301
/network/:site/docs/*           /docs/:splat                                                   301

# The per-site `<site>/watershed` section was renamed to `<site>/environment` (#1254 — it now
# aggregates water + air + toxics, not just the watershed). Keep old/shared per-site links working:
# `:site` captures the one slug segment; the exact rule handles the landing, the splat its subpages.
/network/:site/watershed        /network/:site/environment                                     301
/network/:site/watershed/*      /network/:site/environment/:splat                              301

# The localized labor baseline moved from `<site>/environment/` to `<site>/economy/` (#1893 — the
# Reference menu filed it under Economy, the page declares `section="economy"`, and only its URL
# still said environment). It is cited from the study's labor chapter and from `/ask` answers that
# predate the move, so the old address has to keep resolving. Ordered AFTER the `watershed` rules
# above so the even-older `<site>/watershed/economics-baseline` chains through this one.
/network/:site/environment/economics-baseline   /network/:site/economy/economics-baseline        301

# The dev component showcases used to live in the per-site record tree, then at a network-global
# `/showcase/*` root (#1333). #1894 made them a DEV surface — they build in `astro dev` and are
# absent from the deployed artifact — so the three rules that pointed at them are gone too: a 301
# onto a route production doesn't serve is a slower 404, not a kindness.

# The per-site glossary collapsed into the network-global wiki (#1892 — one taxonomy per noun):
# 75 of its 77 entries were byte-identical at every watershed point, so the concept pages build
# ONCE at /wiki/concepts/ and a record's [[wiki links]] point there. `:site` captures exactly one
# slug segment, so these can't shadow a static route (the old routes are deleted, not real pages).
/network/:site/site/concepts            /wiki/concepts/                                        301
/network/:site/site/concepts/*          /wiki/concepts/:splat                                  301

# The data catalog had two renderings of ONE feed (#1908). `/about/data` (#659) was the static,
# JS-off original; `/about/catalog` (#1000) is the interactive successor and the one the About menu
# carries. Only the successor was ever reachable from the chrome, so the predecessor had become a
# second address for the same page that a reader could reach only from one line of `/about` body
# copy — retired here rather than given a nav slot it would have had to earn twice.
/about/data             /about/catalog                                                         301

# Old network-hub URLs (pre-#402, when the hub was /network). Research is /research/hypotheses.
# Exact rules only — /network/<slug> is real coming-soon content.
#
# ⚠️ There is deliberately NO rule for `/network`: since #1888 that is the real site index (the
# canonical listing of every registered site), served statically. A redirect here would ALWAYS
# fire before the static asset — the page would work in dev, in the build, and in the link check,
# and 301 away in production only. `src/lib/redirects.test.ts` guards that.
/network/hypotheses     /research/hypotheses                                                   301
